#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230086} :activity/id #uuid "a9d8220e-5eea-558d-97c2-fb85d6eeb928" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370 :topic/id #uuid "ef58e174-0df4-568f-8b8f-ebb5d1dacf42" :topic/math-academy-id 6678} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230198 :tutorial/content "Recall that a linear first-order homogeneous differential equation in the variable $y = y(x)$ takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, the equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6xy = 0\n$$\n\nis a linear first-order homogeneous ODE with $a(x) = 1$ and $b(x) = 6x.$ Notice that the right-hand side is identical to zero.\n\nA linear first-order *inhomogeneous* differential equation is similar, except the right-hand side is *not* identical to zero. So, a linear first-order inhomogeneous differential equation can be written as\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is often called the **forcing function.** We say that a first-order ODE has **polynomial forcing** if $f(x)$ is a polynomial.\n\nFor example, the equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6xy = 2x\n$$\n\nis a linear first-order inhomogeneous ODE with polynomial forcing because the right-hand side, $f(x) = 2x,$ is a nonzero polynomial.\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to find general solutions to linear first-order ODEs with polynomial forcing." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c372faf-ce6c-50ce-ba50-f29bb375063c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14545 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4508fb6-525e-5090-a9ae-b0755caac38e" :step/math-academy-id 603073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230175 :tutorial/content "We wish to construct the general solution to the first-order linear inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nThere are several ways to find the general solution to a first-order inhomogeneous ODE. The method we'll discuss here is a three-step process that involves finding the sum of the so-called complementary and particular solutions. \n\nThe three steps are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the **complementary solution** (or the **homogeneous solution**) $y_c(x)$ that satisfies the associated homogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x) + b(x)y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. This is a single function that satisfies the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- **Step 3:** Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution: \n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x).\n$$\n\nAs we'll see, this method generalizes to higher-order linear ODEs, so it's well worth understanding how it works for first-order ODEs.\n\nLet's begin by practicing finding the complementary solution to an equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "eeb7c752-7b59-5c4f-bda0-57bcac65612c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14447 :tutorial/title "A Three-Step Process for Solving First-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "60f91c59-100a-502d-86f9-c9e4e06e6007" :step/math-academy-id 603074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230226 :tutorial/content "Let's find the complementary solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n![](images/f0/f01ce3a6277c6af1c9c8eb4fc1b217b90b3ca72e33a727674c5a1670ccf51f5c.svg)\n\nTo find the complementary solution ![](images/12/12b17ac320da147720f235a1556bbb9bd404f3c8d100cf3b22d145a293d421d3.svg) we solve the corresponding homogeneous ODE.\nWe start by setting the right-hand side equal to zero:\n![](images/5b/5b189ffb1e490e04dc8aea566914128a88e9c48dcc331ff1f5a32ef4af6ee38c.svg)\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n![](images/a8/a8c8cee52474932eb90446a5d3007eea481549ca3be8de61aa32b0bead87e0e2.svg)\n\nWe separate the variables as follows:\n![](images/a0/a0cc584b2c78bd04361fa9ecc0a539f0466fcd22c9727965ff1b33318c542879.svg)\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to ![](images/06/06d9e6ee04f202e0e861c51f0cd11ce47727f565d64d834fa625baea04ae11e6.svg)\n![](images/b9/b962b410044f1e7be26c3bc15cded89ebc0d32a84d712e0f9fab3a953bc4ce5d.svg)\n\nNext, we make ![](images/02/024420a1f4a1300a242618c4742c03a91e27ee710937bb23acd902722711833c.svg) the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n![](images/ec/ec64cfca27de9b893156b962bcb27bd24af86fe03910cd89dbb48ae3c10d1e6d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/bc/bcee7e8a93dffc0dbf0dbd42566a68b0f25841376c48f286dc9670d635318d80.svg)\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the constant to be positive or negative (absorbing the sign into the constant).\nTherefore, the complementary solution is\n![](images/c2/c23ac0e71a5c90e57908d7220af6cc26641787e413a5cd2e1bec7a6a975f752d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an arbitrary constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "739946c6-4a73-5a29-a453-c3a79f65fe12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14698 :tutorial/title "Finding a Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d22c40eb-8457-5361-a39f-e09a1e967559" :step/math-academy-id 603075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228892 :question/id #uuid "cd54adb4-ef2b-5f9d-b920-4ec460bcd199" :question/math-academy-id "e-21611"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363732}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"} :step/id #uuid "0cb4876d-bad8-5565-9a50-5f0d4944b541" :step/math-academy-id 603076 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230179 :tutorial/content "We've discussed how to find the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ of a first-order inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nWe will now turn our attention to finding a **particular solution.**\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ of a linear ODE captures the part of the solution that arises directly from the forcing term $f(x).$\nIn other words, if we substitute $y_p(x)$ into the left-hand side of the equation, it should produce the forcing function $f(x).$\n\nFor equations with constant coefficients, we can often find $y_p(x)$ by looking at the type of function appearing on the right-hand side and then guessing a form that matches it. This is sometimes called **the method of undetermined coefficients.**\n\nFor example, if the forcing function $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $n,$ we guess a particular solution that is also a polynomial of degree $n.$  After making this guess, we substitute it into the differential equation and solve for the unknown constants.\n\nNext, we'll work through a concrete example where $f(x)$ is a linear polynomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "c9c34b5c-1c79-52c1-8f45-ab3604573871" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14457 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d041084b-42d5-550f-9c4b-326eaf35adbd" :step/math-academy-id 603080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230237 :tutorial/content "Consider the inhomogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 5 y = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nHere, the forcing term\n\n$$\nf(x) = 5x - 16\n$$\n\nis a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ so we guess\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nNow substitute $y_p$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha - 5(\\alpha x + \\beta ) = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nNext, group the terms proportional to $x$ and the constants:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x + (\\alpha - 5\\beta) = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients on both sides gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -5\\beta =-16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-1$ and $\\beta=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  -x+3.\n$$\n\nLet's see another example where the forcing function is a quadratic polynomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "40a1be07-f998-5d91-9693-ba72cd0662fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14798 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "6635843c-8657-56a7-b649-c24365b05e2b" :step/math-academy-id 603081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229185 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228893 :question/id #uuid "e0e14ce3-61de-571e-b34e-f56630cc2fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409496}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "a4d952f1-49ab-54a9-a0d5-a77f50eb7f6e" :step/math-academy-id 603082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230180 :tutorial/content "Now that we've practiced finding both the complementary and particular solutions, let's put them together to find the **general solution**.\n\nRecall that the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ comes from solving the associated homogeneous equation. It contains an arbitrary constant, so it represents all homogeneous solutions.\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ is one specific function that satisfies the full inhomogeneous equation.\n\nTo find the general solution, we add these two pieces:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x).\n$$\n\nThe particular solution already satisfies the full equation.\nAdding any complementary solution preserves this property while introducing the arbitrary constant needed to match different initial conditions.\n\nIn this way, the formula above describes *every possible solution* of a linear inhomogeneous equation.\n\nLet's consider an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "fa89e8c5-100d-5467-abfe-c8b867a6de87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14458 :tutorial/title "Constructing the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "636066af-6797-59c7-b358-5bb8f6e14683" :step/math-academy-id 603086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230238 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16.\n$$\n\nWe will find its general solution by computing the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using separation of variables gives\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-2/3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we will find a particular solution. To find a particular solution of\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16,\n$$\n\nnote that the forcing term is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nTherefore, we assume the particular solution is also a constant:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\beta.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ and its derivative into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 0 + 2\\beta = 16.\n$$\n\nSolving yields $\\beta = 8,$ so\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-2/3x} + 8.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fd25308f-b077-5d29-98f3-6c1ad2c59cb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14799 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "f36fce91-06d8-5fe4-a8b5-076c5ddf85e8" :step/math-academy-id 603087 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229186 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228894 :question/id #uuid "344fb3a9-81db-5744-822c-1f989c0cc4de" :question/math-academy-id "e-21613"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409495}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "763320a8-8e20-5ddf-9411-3580fafb3b1f" :step/math-academy-id 603088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228895 :question/id #uuid "f89b7459-5b3d-5da3-a76a-7df3378cb048" :question/math-academy-id "e-21614"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365630}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "0a4bd77e-83fd-5e30-a4b6-14501cd22c8a" :step/math-academy-id 603092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230199 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\na(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove that the sum of the complementary and particular solutions gives a general solution to the equation. \n\nFirst, we note that, by definition, the complementary solution $y_c$ satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_c + b(x)y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nThis can be solved by separating the variables, and the solution is given by (for $a(x)\\neq 0$)\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\exp\\left(-\\int \\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}\\,\\textrm d x\\right).\n$$\n\nBy definition, the particular solution satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe claim that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the most general solution to the equation. \n\n- First, we show that $y_c + y_p$ satisfies the equation. Substituting this into the left-hand side of the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y & =a(x)({y}_{c}^{'}+{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)({y}_{c}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & ={{[a(x){y}_{c}^{'}+b(x){y}_{c}]}_{⏟}}_{0}+[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}] \\\\  & =a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, suppose that $y$ is *any* solution to the equation, and $y_p$ is a known, particular solution. Substituting $y - y_p$ into the left-hand side of the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x)(y-{y}_{p})^{'}+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) & =a(x)({y}^{'}-{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =[a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y]-[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is a solution, we have $a(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x)$, and since $y_p$ is a particular solution, we have $a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\left[a(x)y' + b(x)y\\right] - \\left[a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p\\right]\n= f(x) - f(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, $y - y_p$ satisfies the homogeneous equation, and hence $y - y_p = y_c$. This implies that any solution $y$ is the sum of $y_c$ and $y_p.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c1dca3c-5c18-5c60-a4e4-062b1da7f872" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14549 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Form of the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d734be5c-d8f2-5ce1-99a8-b3ef88363c6f" :step/math-academy-id 603096 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230236 :tutorial/content "Technically, a particular solution $y_p$ is *not unique*.\n\nIf $y_p$ solves\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)\\,y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nand $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)\\,y = 0,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ny_c + y_p\n$$\n\nalso solves the inhomogeneous equation, which means that $y_c + y_p$ is also a particular solution!\n\nHowever, in practice, even though $y_p$ is not unique, it’s very common to use the phrase **“the particular solution”** to mean \"a particular solution that *does not include* any terms from the complementary (homogeneous) solution\".\n\nSo, we typically choose $y_p$ to avoid overlapping with $y_c$, since the full solution is written as\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p.\n$$\n\nThis makes the roles of $y_c$ and $y_p$ clean and non-redundant." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8acee86-4e0b-5fcf-bd6d-8e401a83e24f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14779 :tutorial/title "A Common Convention for “the” Particular Solution"} :step/id #uuid "c87f1941-5836-59f4-967c-4667e6ea5915" :step/math-academy-id 603097}] :activity/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229249}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409500} :activity/id #uuid "0aa93e49-72e3-5515-b5a3-3f4b53ceca51" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370 :topic/id #uuid "ef58e174-0df4-568f-8b8f-ebb5d1dacf42" :topic/math-academy-id 6678} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447d61bd-85be-5a77-92c7-e7b1cbcac24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409486} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e554faf-bf14-5f8b-8095-42b409f87077" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "927f3245-f67b-58d6-852d-475c4b37e55f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332544" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-8.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+4\\beta =-8\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19ee83f6-1de2-5260-a0ac-12e506110b20" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4099c08f-db10-5b94-b476-722ec92e2f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305785} {:answer/id #uuid "424f3742-f4f4-58bf-9771-5a09636ce05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305805} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea666d9-fb1e-528b-9c1a-2a72695635a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306503} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7bafe-af2c-5d92-a1f9-afdc8f293f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306732} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a9087d-0776-582b-a88f-17e01518a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305805} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a523e35-796d-5af3-b102-9fe77f79d844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "da39297b-062f-5af6-a7fc-07a4dc407cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-333042" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -4\\beta )x+(\\beta -4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -4\\beta =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -4\\gamma =-3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "218a75cb-ded3-56d6-8e5a-f6b0268721ec"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a813be27-9e89-5447-a6ed-ccb753aec0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Ae^{-6x}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409488} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea7da04c-6c47-53af-93ba-6b58aa8bcba5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4bc0ce6c-d00c-58b5-b1e7-41739599c291" :question/math-academy-id "q-332541" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=12x+8.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}+2x+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c1d4d31-bdd3-5e06-8ac4-128b5af0e137" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79ad64d0-e59a-5622-ad8f-a7d521d9e58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c92c86-227c-5c65-bc14-31ff63640148" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "abc3945b-d976-54fe-bc38-a631f9c8a6c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332291" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha =8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fac2716-ce9b-5cad-acde-41963ce110a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf2db36-4823-5f0f-a979-18eb641ef853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409489} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc1fdf7c-1bca-53aa-87e0-8b914a783aff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11008462-bb8d-5c30-9ab6-4b789c8f149b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332303" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=12x-8.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x-8\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(\\alpha -6\\beta )=12x-8.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -6\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7fb33d5-37b5-560d-9083-478d43405b96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409499}}] :activity/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409485}]}
